WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:04.800
TINA SRIVASTAVA: I think it's a
really basic point of curiosity

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.580
in the world, and
people that come to MIT,

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.630
who are very passionate and
want to change the world,

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:14.160
should know how airplanes
fly, some information

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.360
about aviation, and the
incredible feat of humankind

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.450
to be able to fly
through the air.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.240
SARAH HANSEN: Today on the
podcast, we're talking flight,

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.060
specifically how a course
has been designed and refined

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.638
to allow hundreds of students
to pursue a path toward flying.

00:00:31.638 --> 00:00:33.930
TINA SRIVASTAVA: I thought
that it was really important

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:36.720
that any student that's
interested in this

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.970
have the opportunity to
learn about airplanes

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:42.418
and learn how to become
a pilot themselves.

00:00:42.418 --> 00:00:44.460
SARAH HANSEN: Welcome to
"Chalk Radio," a podcast

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.620
about inspired teaching at MIT.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:51.150
I'm your host, Sarah Hansen,
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.580
In this episode, we'll be
talking about MIT's flight

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.700
school with two of its
instructors, Philip Greenspun

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.590
and Tina Srivastava.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.440
In our conversation, they share
how a number of innovations

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.510
have made the course
incredibly popular,

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:07.028
both in and outside
of the MIT community.

00:01:07.028 --> 00:01:08.820
Part of why we want to
highlight this class

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.320
is that its
evolution has allowed

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.230
for a nimbleness of
structure without sacrificing

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.990
the effectiveness
of its instruction.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.760
It's truly fascinating
that something

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.070
as seemingly technical
as flight school

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.170
can take so many
different shapes.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:25.410
I wanted to dig into how it's
possible to find flexibility

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.140
in instruction with
such high risks

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.560
and, also, what made this course
so successful at attracting

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.110
and preparing future pilots.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:37.680
We'll pick up our conversation
with Philip and Tina explaining

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.260
what it means to have
access to flight school

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:43.083
and what it really
takes to take flight.

00:01:43.083 --> 00:01:44.500
PHILIP GREENSPUN:
People have been

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.620
wanting to fly for
thousands of years,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.510
and only in the
last 100 years has

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.650
it become a practical reality.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:55.780
And the US is unique, in
that it's half or a third

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:58.330
of the price here and much
easier to do in the US

00:01:58.330 --> 00:01:59.390
than any other country.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:03.903
So as long as people are here
in the US for whatever reason,

00:02:03.903 --> 00:02:05.320
they're never going
to be probably

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.229
more than half an hour's
drive, or an hour's drive,

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:09.759
from a flight school.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:13.150
They're going to
have the ability just

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:15.940
as an ordinary consumer
with a credit card

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:20.380
to spend 5 or 10 hours
mastering the basics of flying.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:24.390
So one thing that I've
tried to stress in the class

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:28.180
is that they don't have to
commit to getting an FAA Pilot

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.220
Certificate, which means
that you're safe to be

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.140
the only pilot in the aircraft.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:33.910
You can go down to
the flight school.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.890
You can get your
hands on the controls,

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:38.770
and within 5 or 10
hours, learn to take off,

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.590
fly around, land without
the instructor having

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:44.620
to touch anything
or say anything,

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:48.340
and that's doable for both
airplanes and helicopters.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.770
Just motivating people to
try to get up in the air

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:57.220
and enjoy this unusual freedom
that we have here in the US.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:00.400
I jumped in as part of
the staff a year ago,

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.820
and I have spent about a month
going through the PowerPoints

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:08.560
to emphasize that this
is an activity that

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:14.340
can be done safely, especially
with a two-pilot crew.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.720
Maybe it's the instructor
and the student,

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.520
or maybe it's a certificated
pilot and another friend

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:23.710
who happens also to be a pilot.

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:26.860
So I did that, and some
of it was just wording.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:30.040
People naturally who are
pilots, unfortunately, they'll

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.090
tend to stress the hazards
and how they avoided them.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:37.703
I think part of it is it makes
them seem more impressive.

00:03:37.703 --> 00:03:39.120
So there were a
lot of PowerPoints

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.970
that says, well, they had
the form of, you will die,

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:45.580
if you will do X. So I
just edited them to say,

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:47.697
you'll be safe, if you do not X.

00:03:47.697 --> 00:03:48.950
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:50.800
PHILIP GREENSPUN: But
it was still hard.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.802
We had a guest lecturer who's
an aerobatics instructor.

00:03:54.802 --> 00:03:55.510
SARAH HANSEN: OK.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.070
PHILIP GREENSPUN:
He spent some time

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:00.580
talking about the hazards
of midair collisions

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:03.760
and how people avoid them,
but if you look at the data,

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.640
that's only 2% of accidents.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.362
It's not a common way to
have an accident at all.

00:04:08.362 --> 00:04:09.070
SARAH HANSEN: OK.

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:11.860
PHILIP GREENSPUN: Why
scare people about it?

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:14.200
Obviously, it's good
to avoid any accident.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:15.040
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.704
PHILIP GREENSPUN: But he didn't
share with the students, look,

00:04:17.704 --> 00:04:20.079
I'm telling you about something
that accounts for only 2%

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:21.130
of accidents.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:22.130
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:23.380
PHILIP GREENSPUN: They're
sitting there terrified

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:25.838
that they'll be flying along,
and all of a sudden, somebody

00:04:25.838 --> 00:04:28.500
will smash into them.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:31.170
SARAH HANSEN: Interesting.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.570
The course format has evolved
from a weekly two-hour course

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:37.270
taking place over
an entire semester

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:40.600
and turned into an
intensive three-day program.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.533
This change has created
more interest in the program

00:04:43.533 --> 00:04:44.950
and allowed more
and more students

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:48.700
to not only pass the FAA exam,
which is the course's intended

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:51.940
goal, but to go on and actually
earn their Private Pilot

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.310
Certificate.

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:55.810
TINA SRIVASTAVA:
So I got engaged

00:04:55.810 --> 00:05:01.510
in the course 16.687, the
Private Pilot Ground School,

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.220
in 2015.

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:08.200
At the time, I was completing
my doctorate at MIT,

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.700
and as we continued teach
the course, over time,

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:15.850
we discussed the
potential of changing

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:19.420
the structure of the
course, and last year

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:23.020
was the first time we changed
the structure of the course.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:25.720
So instead of being
two-hour lectures

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:29.050
in the evenings over the
full spring semester,

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:33.370
it became the three-day
intensive course.

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.140
In the new learning
format, I think

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:40.150
the advantages are that the
students can focus exclusively

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:41.628
on this course for three days.

00:05:41.628 --> 00:05:42.420
SARAH HANSEN: Sure.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:45.070
TINA SRIVASTAVA: So they're not
distracted with other courses

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:47.140
or other activities.

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:50.320
They're just focused and
living and breathing everything

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.060
about becoming a pilot
that's necessary,

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:54.790
and I think that
level of engagement

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:57.190
has a different
set of advantages.

00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:01.090
And so last year was the
pilot of the new format,

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:03.280
and it was very
successful in many ways.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.360
We had about 101 students
on the first day,

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:10.900
and we completed the course
with about 98 students.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.810
That was a big
accomplishment, and we

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:15.520
engaged a lot of
the students again,

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.050
in terms of not only completing
and passing the FAA exam

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:23.470
but continuing that enthusiasm
and getting their Private Pilot

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:25.220
Certificate.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:27.560
SARAH HANSEN: In my conversation
with Philip and Tina,

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:29.240
it became clear that
there was something

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:33.200
important about having
this class meet in person.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:36.500
It seems like exactly the kind
of content that might easily

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:38.970
be converted to
an online format,

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.900
but there was something that
made an in-person gathering not

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.810
just useful but essential here.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:49.060
So I asked them, why
not just do this online?

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:50.710
PHILIP GREENSPUN:
So MIT students

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:55.720
are really good at reading
books, and this material,

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:59.860
to pass the test, you can
self-study successfully.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:01.810
People do that at
commercial flight schools.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:04.510
They just go home, and they
read all the FAA books which

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:07.120
are available online in PDF.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.750
So yes, you have
to ask yourself,

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:13.870
what's the purpose
of even having

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:17.410
lecture halls and a university,
when there is so much available

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:19.900
online, and when you have
a population of people

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:21.610
who are great readers?

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:25.330
And reading is three times
faster than listening,

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:29.620
in terms of just being able
to get through information.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:31.570
So that was part of
why we said, well,

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:34.060
look, we can do
this in three days,

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:38.130
because we're not going
to try to cover everything

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:39.580
and spoon feed it to people.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:40.590
They can read the book.

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:43.290
So we'll ask them to do a
little bit of pre-reading,

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:45.950
and they we'll
inspire them, we hope,

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:49.420
with the in-person
experience, and then

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:51.550
they can go back to the books.

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:54.940
Whereas, I think the
traditional class

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:57.910
assumes that people
don't have access

00:07:57.910 --> 00:07:59.920
to a lot of information
outside of the course.

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:00.490
SARAH HANSEN: I see.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:01.870
PHILIP GREENSPUN:
Or that they have

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:04.453
to be just guided, where they'll
spoon-feed you with a problem

00:08:04.453 --> 00:08:06.610
set or a reading assignment.

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.260
TINA SRIVASTAVA:
Yes, absolutely.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:11.860
I would even say that less
than half of the course

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:16.370
was about the FAA
knowledge of them,

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:18.190
and the reason I
would say that is

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:23.200
that I think that so
much more of the course

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:27.450
was about becoming a pilot.

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:30.330
A lot of the discussion
and the interaction

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:32.850
and the interactive
discussions were really

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:39.960
focused on decision-making which
is very critical for a pilot.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:43.830
Just because you can
answer a factual question

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:47.790
about the weather, for
example, doesn't necessarily

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:53.310
mean you've fully evaluated
whether it is safe to fly,

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:57.270
whether there are alternate
options available, how

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:01.560
you go through that whole
decision-making process.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:06.000
We discuss everything from
aircraft ownership, shared

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:11.310
rentals, flying with
friends, going on vacations,

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:14.100
to really try to
share with students

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:17.190
how they could be
empowered as a pilot,

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:20.190
and we provided a lot
of tips and insight

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:22.810
from our own flying experience.

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:26.580
For example, in the
case of night flying,

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:29.850
the idea of trying to
take off before the sun

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:33.990
has set, so that you begin
to acclimate to the night

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:36.180
environment over time.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:39.570
These types of insights
and experiences

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:42.840
are not part of the
FAA knowledge exam

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:45.420
but were some of the
most important pieces

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:48.650
of the course based on the
student feedback we received.

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:51.060
So it's really about
what the students are

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:53.760
interested in and engaged in.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.340
Throughout the
three-day class, we also

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:00.420
have a number of breaks,
and at every single break,

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:05.160
we would have students come up
and either share an experience

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:08.100
or ask a question or
for clarification.

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:10.230
And that type of
feedback also helps

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:12.940
us inform how we
taught the class

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:15.750
and how we connected different
elements of the course

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:18.810
together to help
answer or provide

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:20.940
some enlightenment
on a particular topic

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:22.500
and how it relates
to other topics.

00:10:26.565 --> 00:10:27.940
SARAH HANSEN: One
of the things I

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:31.060
was most curious about was
how Tina and Philip were

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:35.200
able to bring over 100 students
working at different paces

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:37.180
through this material.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:39.520
These classes are often
so full that there's

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:43.270
a whole other overflow classroom
to accommodate the numbers.

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:45.010
What does it take to do that?

00:10:47.243 --> 00:10:48.910
TINA SRIVASTAVA: One
of the ways that we

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:53.140
engage them is a traditional
method of just asking

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:55.390
a lot of questions,
and we would call up

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:57.930
students to answer them.

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:01.120
So we had a very
engaged group of folks.

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:02.950
Sometimes, we'd asked
questions, where

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:06.420
we'd ask students to raise
their hand to give us an answer,

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:09.472
and sometimes, we'd ask
students to shout out an answer.

00:11:09.472 --> 00:11:10.180
SARAH HANSEN: OK.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:11.638
TINA SRIVASTAVA:
A couple of times,

00:11:11.638 --> 00:11:15.430
we used some FAA
practice exam questions,

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:19.430
and we would have students
raise their hands,

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:22.780
whether they thought
it was A, B, or C.

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:27.430
And depending on how divided
students were on the answer,

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:30.370
we knew whether we needed to
spend more time on the topic

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:31.460
or not.

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:35.140
So for example, one of the
questions that we asked them

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:38.650
was a real question from
the FAA exam and a question

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:43.810
that I got on my own FAA exam
which was about right of way,

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:44.632
when you're flying.

00:11:44.632 --> 00:11:45.340
SARAH HANSEN: OK.

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:47.215
TINA SRIVASTAVA: So it
was, if you're flying,

00:11:47.215 --> 00:11:50.320
and there is an
aircraft refueling

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:54.730
another aircraft, a
glider and a blimp,

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:56.632
which has the right of way?

00:11:56.632 --> 00:11:57.340
SARAH HANSEN: Oh.

00:11:57.340 --> 00:11:59.548
TINA SRIVASTAVA: And this
was a really good question,

00:11:59.548 --> 00:12:02.190
and it generated a lot of
differences of opinion.

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:05.900
We had basically a
split of students

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:08.370
guessing all of three options.

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:11.680
So I'll leave that
interesting question there

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:13.330
without the answer,
so you'll have

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:15.102
to view the course to find out.

00:12:15.102 --> 00:12:16.060
PHILIP GREENSPUN: Yeah.

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:20.320
I guess what I would add is
that it was good to have--

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:24.490
we had fairly frequent coffee
breaks and a pizza break,

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:26.410
but a lot of it was--

00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:29.710
the slide presentation
to the students--

00:12:29.710 --> 00:12:32.260
was really there
to set the stage.

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:35.920
And just having Tina there
with a PhD in aero-astro,

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:38.213
that's way above and
beyond what people

00:12:38.213 --> 00:12:39.880
would have available
in a typical flight

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:42.580
school to really look at
the engineering trade-offs

00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:44.610
instead of just saying,
this is how it works.

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:46.570
Here's your Cessna.

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:50.530
It converged on this design
sometime between the '30s

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:52.210
and '40s.

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:54.700
One good thing about aviation
is there's a huge amount

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:56.570
of government-produced stuff.

00:12:56.570 --> 00:13:02.410
The FAA has done a
good to great job

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:07.090
about making graphics available
for every kind of aviation

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:08.410
topic.

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:14.480
So we mined a lot of this public
domain material from the FAA.

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:16.230
SARAH HANSEN: While
having these materials

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:18.120
accessible in the
public domain can

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:21.750
be a real boon to anyone
teaching complex materials,

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:24.570
it's rarely enough just
to point to the resources

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:27.030
and expect the
knowledge to sink in.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:30.300
I wanted to know how these
elaborate and math-intensive

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:32.790
concepts were conveyed
to students in a way that

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:36.510
empowered them not only to
pass the FAA knowledge test

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:39.570
but to be informed,
practicing pilots.

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:42.660
To conclude our conversation,
I asked Tina and Philip

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:46.140
how they go about creating
clear and useful instruction

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:49.770
on complex topics, and they
told me about a critical lesson

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:54.263
in the class,
weight and balance.

00:13:54.263 --> 00:13:55.930
TINA SRIVASTAVA: For
weight and balance,

00:13:55.930 --> 00:14:00.400
what you're talking about is how
you load the aircraft, in terms

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:03.580
of the fuel, the
passengers, the baggage,

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:06.200
and how that affects the flight.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:08.710
And so we used a
physical model airplane

00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:11.920
to really demonstrate the
effect on an airplane,

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:17.410
if your center of gravity was
too far aft on the airplane.

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:20.920
We also solicited input
from the classroom

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:24.910
regarding their weight
and weights of bags.

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:27.280
We gave them some
insights and tips

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:30.400
that whenever they're flying
to not necessarily trust

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:32.020
what their passengers
tell them is

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:34.600
the weight, their own weight.

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:37.870
So to be safety conscious,
maybe apply a factor of safety

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:40.300
on what your friend tells
you that they weight,

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:44.830
and we used a book to show
how you can do calculations.

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:50.020
And we also went live online to
the website of certain flight

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:54.940
schools to show how you can
find the exact gross weight

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:58.120
and loading characteristics
of different aircraft

00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:00.850
and different online
calculators that

00:15:00.850 --> 00:15:04.880
calculate weight and balance
for you and project that.

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:06.820
And then finally,
we also showed how

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:10.330
to do those calculations on
an iPad with foreign flights.

00:15:10.330 --> 00:15:12.820
So we showed, again,
different ways

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:16.310
to approach the same
information and materials.

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:20.110
So that it wasn't just an
academic description of how

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:22.930
to calculate weight
and balance, but truly

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:25.510
how they would do
that as a pilot,

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:27.880
not just the FAA
regulations for it,

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:30.910
but how they would go about
doing that for a flight

00:15:30.910 --> 00:15:32.756
that they fly on.

00:15:32.756 --> 00:15:36.158
